Pages = {561+},
Month = {JUL 30},
Abstract = {G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) signal primarily through G proteins
   or arrestins. Arrestin binding to GPCRs blocks G protein interaction and
   redirects signalling to numerous G-protein-independent pathways. Here we
   report the crystal structure of a constitutively active form of human
   rhodopsin bound to a pre-activated form of the mouse visual arrestin,
   determined by serial femtosecond X-ray laser crystallography. Together
   with extensive biochemical and mutagenesis data, the structure reveals
   an overall architecture of the rhodopsin-arrestin assembly in which
   rhodopsin uses distinct structural elements, including transmembrane
   helix 7 and helix 8, to recruit arrestin. Correspondingly, arrestin
   adopts the pre-activated conformation, with a similar to 20 degrees
   rotation between the amino and carboxy domains, which opens up a cleft
   in arrestin to accommodate a short helix formed by the second
   intracellular loop of rhodopsin. This structure provides a basis for
   understanding GPCR-mediated arrestin-biased signalling and demonstrates
   the power of X-ray lasers for advancing the frontiers of structural
   biology.},
